%N A401792 Array read by antidiagonals: T(m,n) is the number of nonempty convex vertex sets in the m X n king graph.
%C A401792 A vertex set in a connected graph is convex if for every pair of vertices in the set it contains all other vertices that lie on a shortest path between the two vertices.
%C A401792 The empty set is also convex but is excluded here.
%C A401792 In a king graph, a nonempty convex vertex set corresponds with a set of vertices bounded by two diagonals and two antidiagonals of the grid.
%H A401792 Andrew Howroyd, <a href="/A401792/b401792.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..1275</a> (first 50 antidiagonals)
%H A401792 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/KingGraph.html">King Graph</a>.
%F A401792 T(m,n) = T(n,m).
%F A401792 T(n,k) = (3*(2*k - 1)^2*n^2 + (4*k^3 - 24*k^2 + 32*k - 9)*n - (k - 2)*(k - 1)*(2*k^2 + 2*k - 3))/6 for k <= n.
